## Overview

SunFuel Electric works on the electric vehicle transition. It offers charging along people's daily routes and shares brand stories through partnerships with other companies.

I interned there as a design intern from May to July 2023, in New Delhi. It was an individual project, and I was mentored by George Geo, Design Principal. My task was to redefine SunFuel's visual identity and language and to document it as a brand guideline.

{% callout %}
This project is under a non-disclosure agreement with SunFuel Electric, so I can only show a glimpse of the final identity here.
{% /callout %}

## The brief

The brief was to create a comprehensive brand guideline by redefining the existing visual brand language and identity. The guideline needed to cover logo usage, colour palettes, typography, imagery style and tone of voice, so anyone at SunFuel or a partner could apply the brand without guessing.

## My role

I owned the project from research to the final guideline. I worked with the design and leadership teams, presented concepts to them at each stage and revised the work based on their feedback. I did the identity work, icons and illustrations, and the example applications myself.

## What I made

### Logo system

The logo pairs a yellow sun ring with a "SUNFUEL electric" wordmark. A blue line runs under "electric" and curves up into the word, like a road. I defined a stacked lockup, a horizontal lockup, and the symbol alone for small spaces and app icons.

![SunFuel logo in stacked and horizontal lockups, with the symbol at two sizes](/work/sunfuel-electric/logo-system.webp "Logo lockups and symbol")

### Colour

Yellow and blue are the primary colours, set against a near-black and white. I built a primary gradient from light yellow to orange and a secondary gradient from sky blue to deep blue, plus dark and light background gradients. Extended tint ranges in yellow, blue, purple, green and grey give the system more colours to work with beyond the two primaries.

![Primary colours, gradients and extended tint ranges](/work/sunfuel-electric/colour-palette.webp "Colour palette and gradients")

### Graphic language

I drew a set of simple geometric pieces based on the wordmark's letterforms and repeated them as a pattern. The pattern can sit quietly in a background, as it does on the report cover. A yellow arc taken from the sun ring became a graphic device that can frame a photo or a product.

![Geometric letterform pieces, the repeating pattern and the sun arc](/work/sunfuel-electric/letterforms-and-pattern.webp "Letterform pieces, pattern and the sun arc")

### Icons, layout and illustration

The guideline includes outlined icons in circular frames and a layout grid that shows how headlines, body text and photography fit together. I also drew spot illustrations and a set of characters with a range of skin tones, all in the brand palette.

{% gallery columns=2 %}
![Outlined icons and a layout grid example](/work/sunfuel-electric/icons-and-layout-grid.webp)

![Spot illustrations of a clock and a hand holding cards](/work/sunfuel-electric/spot-illustrations.webp)
{% /gallery %}

![Three standing characters with different skin tones](/work/sunfuel-electric/character-illustrations.webp "Character illustrations")

### Applications

To test the system on real formats, I made ads for an E-Trails offer, "EV users, stay for free", in banner and square sizes. They use the sun arc over the photograph, the brand type and the light background gradient.

![E-Trails banner ad, "EV users, stay for free"](/work/sunfuel-electric/etrails-ad-banner.webp "Ad application, banner")

![E-Trails square ad on the dark background gradient](/work/sunfuel-electric/etrails-ad-square.webp "Ad application, square")

## Process & decisions

I planned the ten weeks in five stages:

- **Onboarding:** working through the project brief
- **Research and planning:** understanding SunFuel's vision and values, market research and brand positioning
- **Concept development:** brand strategy and creating design concepts
- **Design refinement:** presenting concepts, working on feedback and developing collateral
- **Brand guideline:** organising the final elements and their usage rules, then finalising and delivering

![Ten-week project timeline](/work/sunfuel-electric/project-timeline.webp "Project timeline")

Research and positioning came before any visual work, so the concepts I presented were grounded in SunFuel's vision and values. Looking at the final system, two shapes carry most of it: the sun ring and the curved line under "electric". The arc device, the gradients and the pattern all come from them, which keeps the identity simple to apply.

Collateral was developed during refinement, alongside the feedback rounds, rather than after the guideline was done. That way the elements were already tested on ads and layouts before I wrote their usage rules.

## Outcome & learnings

I delivered the final brand guideline at the end of the ten weeks. The details stay private under the NDA, and I don't have data on how it has been used since.

What I took away is that a guideline is only as useful as the decisions behind it. Writing usage rules made me explain choices I had made by instinct, and the ones I couldn't explain usually needed another look. Presenting concepts to the design and leadership teams and working through their feedback was also a big part of the project, and good practice in explaining design decisions to people outside design.
